# This tree is published. Everything below holds a secret, points at our deployment, or is build
# output, and none of it belongs in a public repo.
#
# The publish script (publish-selfhost.sh) works from an allowlist and doesn't rely on this file:
# two independent mechanisms, because one being wrong once puts a live database id on the
# internet.

# The real deployment configs, carrying our hosted deployment's live D1 database ids. Untracked
# on purpose and kept on disk so `wrangler deploy` works from a dev machine. A fresh clone
# recreates them from the tracked wrangler.example.jsonc.
messaging-worker/wrangler.jsonc
files-worker/wrangler.jsonc

# Throwaway configs for the custom-servers end-to-end run.
messaging-worker/wrangler.test.jsonc
files-worker/wrangler.test.jsonc

# Local secrets: APNs signing keys, admin tokens, Turnstile keys.
.dev.vars
**/.dev.vars

# A filled-in on-prem config holds ADMIN_TOKEN and the enrollment hashes.
# config.capnp.example is tracked; this is the copy an operator edits.
# Under Docker it's derived at container start and never written to the host.
config.capnp

# The Docker deployment's two. .env.example is tracked; .env is the operator's copy, and
# secrets.env holds the generated admin token and durable-object keys.
.env
secrets.env

# Throwaway workerd configs written by the test scripts.
.test-*.capnp

# Build output: bundles and the generated capnp module lists.
dist/

# Dependencies, and wrangler's local state.
node_modules/
.wrangler/
.wrangler-dev-state/
# Second local state dir, for running both Workers side by side (files on 8787, messaging on
# 8788). One can't serve both: the D1 binding name is the same.
.wrangler-dev-state-msg/

# Durable-object state and blobs, if someone runs a box inside the checkout.
do-messaging/
do-files/
blobs/

.DS_Store
*.log
